---
/* The wordmark is the one place a personal site earns its accent: a name in a
   hand font is a signature, and colouring it is the smallest possible way to
   say the palette out loud.

   Ticket 13 finishes the thought: a signature is an act, not a glyph, so it
   signs itself on every load and a dwell on the finished name signs it again.
   That means the name can no longer be a text node — you cannot draw a font
   file — so the four letterforms below are Excalifont's own outlines, traced
   from `src/fonts/Excalifont-Regular.woff2` with fontTools, the same way the
   favicon was made. Same glyphs, same advances (328 / 576 / 543 / 543, no
   kerning in the font), same fill via `currentColor`. A different hand writing
   his name would be a forgery.

   The box is the old h1's box, reconstructed rather than approximated:
   `text-display` is 2.75rem with line-height 1, and with Excalifont's 886/374
   metrics on a 1000upm grid the baseline of that 1em line box sits 756 units
   from its top. Hence `viewBox="0 -756 1990 1000"` and `height: 1em` — the
   letters land on the same pixels the text did, and nothing shifts. The
   descender of the j overflowed the old line box; `overflow: visible` lets it
   keep doing that.

   How the signing works, because it is not the obvious dash trick: a
   handwriting font is filled outlines, and animating stroke-dashoffset on an
   outline traces a hollow letter — an intro animation, not a pen. Instead each
   letter's fill sits behind a mask, and inside the mask the same outline is
   STROKED, fat (150 units, roughly twice the pen width), with the dash
   animation running on that. The fat stroke's leading edge sweeps along the
   outline and the fill appears behind it — ink coming off a nib. Each contour
   was rotated to start where a pen would start (top of the j's stem, the top
   right of each s), so the reveal runs in writing order, and the j's dot is
   last in its path on purpose: the word finishes, then the j gets dotted,
   which is how people actually sign.

   Every mask also holds a plain white rect. That rect IS the static
   signature: with JS off, or with reduced motion, or before the script runs,
   the masks pass everything and this is exactly the filled wordmark it always
   was. The script only ever toggles one class; everything it enables is
   animation, and everything the page needs is already in the markup. */
---

<h1 class="font-hand text-display text-accent">
  <span class="sr-only">jass</span>
  <svg
    class="sig"
    viewBox="0 -756 1990 1000"
    aria-hidden="true"
    fill="currentColor">
    {
      /* The j and the a are each two subpaths — stem and dot, ring and its
      counter — and they are kept as SEPARATE defs because the pens must dash
      them separately: dash behaviour across subpaths is the least agreed-on
      corner of SVG, and the first renderer this was tested in drew the dot
      before the stem. One pen, one subpath, nothing to disagree about. */
    }
    <defs>
      <path
        id="wm-js"
        d="M166 -316Q208 -289 212 -253Q260 -38 285 241Q290 305 259 349Q221 376 171 376Q130 376 69 355Q32 341 -47 290Q-69 280 -80 256Q-90 233 -85 208Q-79 190 -57 177Q-35 164 -13 164Q1 164 10 170Q16 179 24 202Q33 225 34 227Q103 282 153 282Q184 282 196 260Q205 200 185 76Q165 -47 164 -62Q160 -82 153 -128Q146 -175 138 -212Q131 -248 120 -277Q119 -292 135 -304Q151 -317 166 -316Z"
      ></path>
      <path
        id="wm-jd"
        d="M175 -415Q154 -417 118 -439Q83 -461 79 -463Q41 -495 51 -550Q73 -583 95 -583Q126 -583 130 -579Q136 -574 146 -565Q157 -556 162 -550Q168 -545 174 -536Q181 -528 183 -519Q186 -514 192 -502Q199 -490 202 -483Q206 -476 210 -466Q214 -455 216 -446Q216 -430 204 -420Q191 -411 175 -415Z"
      ></path>
      <path
        id="wm-ao"
        d="M523 7Q492 11 459 -2Q426 -14 407 -37Q398 -51 394 -56Q391 -62 384 -74Q377 -87 374 -95Q372 -103 369 -114Q366 -125 366 -136Q366 -146 369 -157Q370 -191 392 -226Q413 -261 435 -282Q457 -303 492 -332Q499 -337 502 -340Q544 -374 616 -374Q631 -374 711 -366Q713 -364 724 -359Q736 -354 741 -348Q746 -343 742 -334L755 -321Q777 -308 783 -296Q792 -281 816 -202Q818 -194 836 -148Q853 -102 855 -93Q880 -47 881 -32L886 -16Q885 -11 877 -5Q869 1 868 2Q851 8 816 -4Q809 -7 804 -12Q798 -16 796 -20Q793 -23 786 -33Q780 -43 777 -48L773 -53Q762 -79 739 -145Q699 -77 642 -34Q585 8 523 7Z"
      ></path>
      {
        /* The a again, whole — outer ring and its counter in one path, because
        a hole only exists when both windings share a fill. The pen strokes
        `wm-ao`; the page fills `wm-a`. The one duplicated outline on the
        site, and the mask bug above is why it stays duplicated. */
      }
      <path
        id="wm-a"
        d="M523 7Q492 11 459 -2Q426 -14 407 -37Q398 -51 394 -56Q391 -62 384 -74Q377 -87 374 -95Q372 -103 369 -114Q366 -125 366 -136Q366 -146 369 -157Q370 -191 392 -226Q413 -261 435 -282Q457 -303 492 -332Q499 -337 502 -340Q544 -374 616 -374Q631 -374 711 -366Q713 -364 724 -359Q736 -354 741 -348Q746 -343 742 -334L755 -321Q777 -308 783 -296Q792 -281 816 -202Q818 -194 836 -148Q853 -102 855 -93Q880 -47 881 -32L886 -16Q885 -11 877 -5Q869 1 868 2Q851 8 816 -4Q809 -7 804 -12Q798 -16 796 -20Q793 -23 786 -33Q780 -43 777 -48L773 -53Q762 -79 739 -145Q699 -77 642 -34Q585 8 523 7ZM518 -86Q535 -86 565 -94Q614 -112 654 -157Q693 -211 698 -252Q698 -255 702 -276Q706 -296 705 -304Q685 -299 648 -296Q610 -294 590 -290Q564 -286 537 -266Q510 -245 496 -227Q482 -209 458 -176Q441 -155 464 -113Q482 -86 518 -86Z"
      ></path>
      <path
        id="wm-s"
        d="M376 -361Q371 -361 344 -360Q317 -359 309 -358Q301 -358 279 -356Q257 -353 245 -350Q233 -347 217 -341Q201 -335 187 -327Q173 -322 168 -311Q164 -300 180 -292Q227 -260 317 -217Q407 -174 439 -156Q528 -100 528 -34Q528 60 301 68Q251 70 194 53Q137 36 97 5Q57 -10 66 -70Q68 -74 75 -85Q82 -96 85 -102Q137 -93 173 -58Q188 -46 205 -39Q222 -32 244 -30Q265 -27 279 -26Q293 -25 320 -26Q346 -26 357 -26Q385 -26 414 -37Q424 -41 426 -52Q428 -63 420 -70Q401 -84 322 -123Q242 -162 237 -165Q151 -192 85 -246Q80 -253 68 -262Q57 -272 50 -282Q43 -291 41 -303Q40 -342 79 -372L142 -412Q235 -449 319 -455Q321 -455 330 -454Q340 -453 342 -453Q343 -453 351 -452Q359 -451 361 -450Q363 -449 369 -447Q375 -445 378 -442Q380 -440 384 -436Q388 -433 390 -428Q393 -424 395 -418Q401 -383 376 -361Z"
      ></path>
    </defs>

    {
      /* userSpaceOnUse because the default mask region is a percentage box
      that would clip the descender and the fat pen stroke; the explicit
      rect is generous for the same reason. */
    }
    <mask
      id="wm-mj"
      maskUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
      x="-300"
      y="-800"
      width="2600"
      height="1300">
      <rect
        class="flood"
        x="-300"
        y="-800"
        width="2600"
        height="1300"
        fill="#fff"></rect>
      <use class="pen pen-j" href="#wm-js"></use>
      <use class="pen pen-jd" href="#wm-jd"></use>
    </mask>
    <mask
      id="wm-ma"
      maskUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
      x="-300"
      y="-800"
      width="2600"
      height="1300">
      <rect
        class="flood"
        x="-300"
        y="-800"
        width="2600"
        height="1300"
        fill="#fff"></rect>
      <use class="pen pen-a" href="#wm-ao"></use>
    </mask>
    {
      /* Both s masks hold their pen at the origin, and the advance happens
      outside, on the group wrapping the fill — a transform on mask content
      broke the mask outright in the one renderer this was testable in, and
      a mask evaluated in the fill's own translated space needs none. */
    }
    <mask
      id="wm-ms1"
      maskUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
      x="-300"
      y="-800"
      width="2600"
      height="1300">
      <rect
        class="flood"
        x="-300"
        y="-800"
        width="2600"
        height="1300"
        fill="#fff"></rect>
      <use class="pen pen-s1" href="#wm-s"></use>
    </mask>
    <mask
      id="wm-ms2"
      maskUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
      x="-300"
      y="-800"
      width="2600"
      height="1300">
      <rect
        class="flood"
        x="-300"
        y="-800"
        width="2600"
        height="1300"
        fill="#fff"></rect>
      <use class="pen pen-s2" href="#wm-s"></use>
    </mask>

    <g mask="url(#wm-mj)">
      <use href="#wm-js"></use>
      <use href="#wm-jd"></use>
    </g>
    <use href="#wm-a" mask="url(#wm-ma)"></use>
    <g transform="translate(904)">
      <use href="#wm-s" mask="url(#wm-ms1)"></use>
    </g>
    <g transform="translate(1447)">
      <use href="#wm-s" mask="url(#wm-ms2)"></use>
    </g>
  </svg>
</h1>

<script is:inline>
  /* Inline, not bundled, for one reason: the signing must be armed before
     first paint. A deferred module is a separate request, and on a cold cache
     the page would paint the finished signature, then snatch it back and
     redraw it — a re-sign, not an entrance. This runs during parse, right
     after the h1 above it exists.

     Armed, not started. `signing` alone holds the pens paused at their start;
     the animation clock only runs once `go` lands, one rAF later — on the
     first frame anyone actually sees. Without that, the clock starts during
     parse and whatever the parser spends before paint is spent eating the j.
     rAF also does not tick in a hidden tab, which falls out as exactly right:
     a link opened in the background signs when the reader first looks at it,
     not alone in the dark.

     Two classes are the only thing JS touches. If this never runs, the markup
     is the finished signature; if the animations never fire (browsers may
     freeze them under load), the timeout restores it. The name must not be
     losable to an edge case. */
  (() => {
    var sig = document.querySelector(".sig");
    if (!sig) return;
    var safety;
    var sign = function () {
      if (sig.classList.contains("signing")) return;
      sig.classList.add("signing");
      requestAnimationFrame(function () {
        sig.classList.add("go");
        clearTimeout(safety);
        safety = setTimeout(function () {
          sig.classList.remove("signing", "go");
        }, 1000);
      });
    };
    /* the s2 pen finishes last (635ms); its end is the pen lifting off */
    sig.addEventListener("animationend", function (e) {
      if (e.target.classList && e.target.classList.contains("pen-s2")) {
        clearTimeout(safety);
        sig.classList.remove("signing", "go");
      }
    });
    sig.addEventListener("resign", sign);
    if (!matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches) sign();
  })();
</script>

<script>
  /* The re-sign goes through the friend, so the wordmark keeps the site's
     dwell grammar — 500ms on pointer, scroll-dwell on touch — instead of
     inventing a hover of its own. No `once`: signing again on request is the
     point, and reduced motion is the friend's problem, already solved. */
  import { ambient } from "@/scripts/friend";

  const sig = document.querySelector("svg.sig");
  if (sig) {
    ambient({ el: sig, act: () => sig.dispatchEvent(new Event("resign")) });
  }
</script>

<style>
  .sig {
    display: block;
    height: 1em;
    width: auto;
    /* the j's descender overflowed the old line box; still does */
    overflow: visible;
  }

  /* The pens live inside the masks and are never visible themselves — white
     is mask language for "show", not a colour. Width 150 on letterforms whose
     pen stroke measures ~80–87 units: fat enough that one pass of the outline
     reveals the full stroke behind it, thin enough that the stem's pass stops
     19 units short of the dot, which therefore stays undrawn until its own
     turn. */
  .pen {
    fill: none;
    stroke: #fff;
    stroke-width: 150;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
  }

  /* Everything below exists only while `.signing` is set, and only for
     readers who have not asked for less motion — for everyone else the flood
     rects pass the masks whole and the block compiles to nothing happening.

     The dash lengths are the measured outline lengths padded ~3%, because the
     browser's own arc-length arithmetic will not equal fontTools' and a dash
     that comes up short leaves a bitten letter. The keyframe percentages are
     arc positions at constant pen speed (~8.7 units/ms, the whole word in
     ~620ms — measured against the ticket's "600ms feels like a pen"):

       j    0–128ms   stem, top to bottom, round the hook
       a    130–273ms bowl and tail in one stroke
       s    260–405ms top curve, through the middle, out the bottom
       s    405–550ms again, offset an advance
       j    550–620ms the dot — the word finishes, then the j is dotted

     Each pen's remaining timeline is its outline's return edge, which paints
     almost nothing new — it reads as the ink settling, and the next letter
     starts over it rather than waiting. `linear`, because within one stroke
     a pen does not ease. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .signing .flood {
      opacity: 0;
    }
    /* armed but not started: pens hold at their first frame until the script
       has seen a paint (the `go` class, one rAF after `signing`) */
    .signing:not(.go) .pen {
      animation-play-state: paused;
    }
    .signing .pen-j {
      stroke-dasharray: 2160;
      stroke-dashoffset: 2160;
      animation: wm-sign-j 300ms linear both;
    }
    .signing .pen-jd {
      stroke-dasharray: 540;
      stroke-dashoffset: 540;
      animation: wm-sign-out 70ms linear 550ms both;
    }
    .signing .pen-a {
      stroke-dasharray: 1685;
      stroke-dashoffset: 1685;
      animation: wm-sign-a 200ms linear 130ms both;
    }
    .signing .pen-s1 {
      stroke-dasharray: 2730;
      stroke-dashoffset: 2730;
      animation: wm-sign-s 230ms linear 260ms both;
    }
    .signing .pen-s2 {
      stroke-dasharray: 2730;
      stroke-dashoffset: 2730;
      animation: wm-sign-s 230ms linear 405ms both;
    }

    @keyframes wm-sign-j {
      42.6% {
        stroke-dashoffset: 1037;
      }
      to {
        stroke-dashoffset: 0;
      }
    }
    @keyframes wm-sign-a {
      71.5% {
        stroke-dashoffset: 435;
      }
      to {
        stroke-dashoffset: 0;
      }
    }
    @keyframes wm-sign-out {
      to {
        stroke-dashoffset: 0;
      }
    }
    @keyframes wm-sign-s {
      63% {
        stroke-dashoffset: 1457;
      }
      to {
        stroke-dashoffset: 0;
      }
    }
  }
</style>
